.TH SCREENATOR 1 "October 27, 2026"
.SH NAME
Screenator \- vibe-coded screen annotation tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B screenator
.RI [ options ]
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBScreenator\fP lets you draw annotations over all your running applications.
It can run in the background and be activated on demand. The drawing stays on
screen as long as you want, and you can continue to use your applications
while it is visible.
.br
\fBScreenator\fP is a fork of Gromit-MPX 1.4.2. It adds a small draggable
on-screen icon whose left/right click opens a menu with the drawing tools
(pen, marker, shapes, text and eraser) and the settings (line colour,
thickness, eraser size, opacity, shape fill, text font and floating icon
size). The same menu is available from the system tray icon.
.br
The Marker draws a yellow highlighter stroke with a fixed 32 pixel width and
50% opacity. Its settings are independent of the line colour, thickness and
opacity chosen for the other tools.
.br
The Shapes submenu contains the Marker, Free Line, Arrow, Angled Line, Angled
Arrow, Rectangle, Ellipse and Rounded Rectangle tools. Shapes are drawn by
dragging from the start point to the end point; the free line and the arrow
follow the drag direction, while the Angled Line and Angled Arrow snap to 0,
30, 45, 60, 90, 120, 135 and 150 degrees. The arrow tools add a head at the
end point. Rectangles, ellipses and rounded rectangles can be filled using
the "Shapes Fill Color" submenu; the default is no fill.
.br
The eraser size can be set to 20, 40 or 60 pixels from the menu. The erasing
area and the circular eraser cursor both match the selected size.
.br
The Text tool lets you write directly on the screen. Click where the text
should start and type: a small borderless editor appears at that position,
using the pen colour and the font selected by "Change Font". Press Enter to
place the text or Escape to cancel it.
.br
The pen and font settings are remembered in
.I ~/.config/screenator.ini
and can be restored to their defaults with the "Reset Settings" menu entry.
.br
.SH KEYBOARD CONTROL
By default, Screenator grabs the "F9" key (this can be changed using the
"\-\-key" option), making it unavailable to other applications. The
available shortcuts are:
.TP
.B F9
toggle painting
.TP
.B SHIFT-F9
clear screen
.TP
.B CTRL-F9
toggle visibility
.TP
.B ALT-F9
quit Screenator
.TP
.B F8
undo last stroke
.TP
.B SHIFT-F8
redo last undone stroke
.PP
.SH OPTIONS (STARTUP)
A short summary of the available command line arguments for starting Screenator;
see below for the options to control an already running Screenator process:
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-active
start Screenator and immediately activate it.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-debug
gives some debug output.
.TP
.B \-k <keysym>, \-\-key <keysym>
change the key used to grab the mouse. <keysym> can e.g. be "F9", "F12",
"Control_R" or "Print". To determine the keysym for different keys you can use
the \fBxev\fP(1) command. You can specify "none" to prevent Screenator from
grabbing a key.
.TP
.B \-K <keycode>, \-\-keycode <keycode>
change the key used to grab the mouse by keycode instead of keysym.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-opacity <value>
set the initial drawing opacity using a value between 0 and 1.
.TP
.B \-u <keysym>, \-\-undo\-key <keysym>
change the key used to undo/redo strokes.
.TP
.B \-U <keycode>, \-\-undo\-keycode <keycode>
change the undo/redo key by keycode instead of keysym.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
show the Screenator version.
.SH OPTIONS (CONTROL)
A short summary of the available command line arguments to control an already
running Screenator process:
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-clear
clear the screen.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quit
cause the running Screenator process to quit.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-toggle
toggle the grabbing of the pointer.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-visibility
toggle the visibility of the drawing.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-reload
reload the tool configuration.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-redo
redo the last undone drawing stroke.
.TP
.B \-z, \-\-undo
undo the last drawing stroke.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
Screenator uses this to determine which desktop environment it is running on.
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_SESSION
Screenator uses this to determine whether it is running under X11 or Wayland.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory to search for the user's configuration files, defaults to
.BI ~ /.config/ .
.TP
.B GDK_CORE_DEVICE_EVENTS
If set, GDK does not use the XInput extension and only reacts to core X input
events, which renders Screenator unusable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/screenator.cfg
User tool configuration: defines pens and maps mouse buttons and modifiers to
them.
.TP
.I /etc/screenator/screenator.cfg
System-wide tool configuration, used when the user configuration does not
exist.
.TP
.I ~/.config/screenator.ini
Saved menu settings (line colour, thickness, opacity, shape fill, floating
icon size, text font and the selected tool).
.SH NOTES
Screenator is X11-only. Under Wayland it runs through XWayland.
